00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:04.330
In the last video, you saw the basic beam surge algorithm.

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:10.490
In this video, you learned some little changes that make it work even better.

00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:14.410
Length normalization is a small change to the beam surge algorithm that can help you

00:00:14.410 --> 00:00:16.090
get much better results.

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:17.950
Here's what it is.

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:23.010
We talked about beam surge as maximizing this probability.

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:33.770
This product here is just expressing the observation that P of y1 up to yty given x can be expressed

00:00:33.770 --> 00:00:49.010
as P of y1 given x times P of y2 given x and y1 times dot dot dot up to I guess P of yty

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:56.630
given x and y1 up to yty minus 1.

00:00:56.630 --> 00:01:01.590
Maybe this notation is a bit more scary and more intimidating than it needs to be, but this

00:01:01.590 --> 00:01:07.390
is that probability, product of probabilities that you've seen previously.

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:13.350
Now if you're implementing these, these probabilities are all numbers less than 1.

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:16.230
In fact, often they're much less than 1.

00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:21.850
And multiplying a lot of numbers less than 1 will result in a tiny tiny tiny number which

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:27.570
can result in numerical underflow, meaning that it's too small for the floating part representation

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:31.650
in your computer to store accurately.

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:40.210
So in practice, instead of maximizing this product, we will take logs.

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:47.230
And if you insert a log there, then the log of a product becomes a sum of a log and maximizing

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:52.950
this sum of log probabilities should give you the same result in terms of selecting the

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:55.750
most likely sentence y.

00:01:55.750 --> 00:02:01.790
So by taking logs, you end up with a more numerically stable algorithm that is less

00:02:01.790 --> 00:02:09.750
prone to rounding errors, numerical rounding errors or to really numerical underflow.

00:02:09.770 --> 00:02:14.930
And because the logarithmic function, here's the log function, is a strictly monotonically

00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:21.970
increasing function, we know that maximizing log p of y given x should give you the same

00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:26.930
result as maximizing p of y given x.

00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:32.950
As in the same value of y that maximizes this should also maximize that.

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:38.330
So in most implementations, you keep track of the sum of logs of the probabilities

00:02:38.350 --> 00:02:41.590
rather than the product of the probabilities.

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:48.710
Now there's one other change to this objective function that makes the machine translation

00:02:48.710 --> 00:02:57.210
algorithm work even better, which is that if you refer to this original objective up

00:02:57.210 --> 00:03:02.970
here, if you have a very long sentence, the probability of that sentence is going to

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:08.330
be low because you're multiplying as many terms here, lots of numbers are less than

00:03:08.330 --> 00:03:11.630
one to estimate the probability of that sentence.

00:03:11.630 --> 00:03:15.830
And so if you multiply a lot of numbers, less than one together, you just tend to end

00:03:15.830 --> 00:03:20.030
up with a smaller probability.

00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:25.470
And so this objective function has an undesirable effect that it maybe

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:31.070
unnaturally tends to prefer very short translations, tends to prefer very short

00:03:31.070 --> 00:03:33.270
outputs.

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:37.550
Because the probability of a short sentence is determined just by multiplying,

00:03:37.570 --> 00:03:42.240
fewer of these numbers are less than one.

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:46.600
And so the product will just be not quite as small.

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:49.040
And by the way, the same thing is true for this.

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:55.040
The log of a probability is always less than or equal to one.

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:57.120
You're actually in this range of a log.

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:04.880
So the more terms you add together, the more negative this thing becomes.

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:08.600
So there's one other change to the algorithm that makes it work better,

00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:15.100
which is instead of using this as the objective you're trying to maximize,

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:20.740
one thing you could do is normalize this by the number of words in your translation.

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:26.620
And so this takes the average of the log of the probability of each word.

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:34.420
Now, and this significantly reduces the penalty for outputting longer translations.

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:40.120
And in practice, as a heuristic, instead of dividing by t y, but the number of words

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:45.440
in the output sentence, sometimes you use the softer approach where you have t y to the power

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:50.160
of alpha, where maybe alpha is equal to 0.7.

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:54.640
So if alpha was equal to one, then you're completely normalizing by length.

00:04:54.640 --> 00:05:00.040
If alpha was equal to zero, then well, t y to the zero would be one.

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:02.160
Then you're just not normalizing at all.

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:06.820
And this is somewhere in between full normalization and no normalization.

00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:12.620
And alpha is another parameter, hyperparameter of the algorithm that you can tune to try

00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:15.780
to get the best results.

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:21.100
And have to admit, using alpha this way, this is a heuristic or this is a hack.

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:26.900
There isn't a great theoretical justification for it, but people found that this works well.

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:30.860
People found that it works well in practice, so many groups will do this.

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:37.040
And you can try out different values of alpha until and then see which one gives you the best result.

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:44.800
So just to wrap up how you run beam search, as you run beam search, you see a lot of sentences

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:50.560
with length equal one, lot of sentences with length equal two, lot of sentences with length

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:53.480
equals three, and so on.

00:05:53.480 --> 00:05:56.040
And maybe you run beam search for 30 steps.

00:05:56.040 --> 00:06:01.420
You consider output sentences up to length 30, let's say.

00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:08.460
And so with beam with a three, you would be keeping track of the top three possibilities

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:15.540
for each of these possible sentence lengths, one, two, three, four, and so on up to 30.

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:26.890
Then you would look at all the output sentences and score them against the score.

00:06:26.890 --> 00:06:33.990
And so you can take your top sentences and just compute this objective function on the

00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:39.270
sentences that you have seen through the beam search process.

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:44.530
And then finally, of all these sentences that you evaluate this way, you would pick the

00:06:44.530 --> 00:06:50.750
one that achieves the highest value on this normalized log probability objective.

00:06:50.750 --> 00:06:54.830
Sometimes it's called a normalized log likelihood objective, and then that would be the final

00:06:54.830 --> 00:06:58.090
translation, the output.

00:06:58.090 --> 00:07:00.450
So that's how you implement beam search.

00:07:00.450 --> 00:07:04.370
And you get to play this yourself in this week's programming exercise.

00:07:04.370 --> 00:07:07.890
Finally, a few implementation details.

00:07:07.890 --> 00:07:10.470
How do you choose the beam with?

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:15.910
So here are the pros and cons of setting B to be very large versus very small.

00:07:15.910 --> 00:07:22.390
If the beam width is very large, then you consider a lot of possibilities.

00:07:22.390 --> 00:07:28.010
And so you tend to get a better result because you're considering a lot of different options,

00:07:28.010 --> 00:07:30.130
but it will be slower.

00:07:30.130 --> 00:07:35.770
And the memory requirements will also grow, but also be computationally slower.

00:07:35.770 --> 00:07:40.290
Whereas if you use a very small beam width, then you get a worse result because you're

00:07:40.290 --> 00:07:46.610
just keeping less possibilities in mind as the algorithm is running, but you get a

00:07:46.610 --> 00:07:51.490
result faster, and the memory requirements will also be lower.

00:07:51.490 --> 00:07:56.650
So in the previous video, we used in our running example a beam width of three.

00:07:56.650 --> 00:07:59.510
So we're keeping three possibilities in mind.

00:07:59.510 --> 00:08:02.110
In practice, that is on the small side.

00:08:02.110 --> 00:08:07.670
In production systems, it's not uncommon to see a beam width maybe around 10.

00:08:07.670 --> 00:08:13.310
And I think a beam width of 100 would be considered very large for a production system,

00:08:13.310 --> 00:08:14.950
depending on the application.

00:08:14.950 --> 00:08:20.270
But for research systems where people want to squeeze out every last drop of performance

00:08:20.330 --> 00:08:25.350
in order to publish a paper, the best possible result is not uncommon to see people use beam

00:08:25.350 --> 00:08:28.070
widths of a thousand or three thousand.

00:08:28.070 --> 00:08:33.570
But this is very application as well as domain dependent.

00:08:33.570 --> 00:08:39.570
So I would say try out a variety of values of B and see what works for your application.

00:08:39.570 --> 00:08:45.970
But when B gets very large, there is often diminishing returns.

00:08:45.970 --> 00:08:51.250
So for many applications, I would expect to see a huge gain as you go from beam

00:08:51.250 --> 00:08:55.110
width of one, which is basically greedy search to three to maybe 10.

00:08:55.110 --> 00:09:01.550
But the gains as you go from a thousand to three thousand in beam width might not be as big.

00:09:01.550 --> 00:09:08.590
And for those of you that have taken maybe a lot of computer science courses before,

00:09:08.590 --> 00:09:12.830
if you're familiar with computer science search algorithms like BFS,

00:09:12.830 --> 00:09:17.870
breadth of search or DFS, depth of search, the way to think about beam search is that

00:09:17.870 --> 00:09:22.070
unlike those other algorithms, which you might have learned about in a computer science

00:09:22.070 --> 00:09:26.030
algorithms course, and don't worry about it if you've not heard of these algorithms,

00:09:26.030 --> 00:09:30.270
but if you've heard of breadth of search or depth of search, then unlike those algorithms,

00:09:30.270 --> 00:09:36.350
which are exact search algorithms, beam search runs much faster, but does not guarantee to

00:09:36.350 --> 00:09:41.630
find the exact maximum for this argument you like to find.

00:09:41.630 --> 00:09:44.750
If you haven't heard of breadth of search or depth of search, don't worry about it.

00:09:44.750 --> 00:09:46.710
It's not important for our purposes.

00:09:46.710 --> 00:09:51.710
But if you have, this is how beam search relates to those algorithms.

00:09:51.710 --> 00:09:57.310
So that's it for beam search, which is a widely used algorithm in many production systems

00:09:57.310 --> 00:09:59.950
or in many commercial systems.

